ctor receptor in tumour angiogenesis</article-title><source>Hematol Oncol Clin North Am</source><year>2004</year><volume>18</volume><fpage>1007</fpage><lpage>27</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15474332</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="CIT29"><label>29</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tabernero</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The role of VEGF and EGFR inhibition: Implications for combining anti- VEGF and anti EGFR agents</article-title><source>Mol Cancer Res</source><year>2007</year><volume>5</volume><fpage>203</fpage><lpage>20</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17374728</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="CIT30"><label>30</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Krebs</surname><given-names>LJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>X</given-names></name><name><surname>Pudavar</surname><given-names>HE</given-names></name><name><surname>Bergey</surname><given-names>EJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Schally</surname><given-names>AV</given-names></name><name><surname>Nagy</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Regulation of targeted chemotherapy with cytotoxic LH-RH analog by epidermal growth factor</article-title><source>Cancer Res</source><year>2000</year><volume>60</volume><fpage>4194</fpage><lpage>9</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10945629</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="CIT31"><label>31</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ben-Yehudah</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Lorberboun-Galski</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Targeted cancer therapy with gonadotropin-releasing hormone chimeric proteins</article-title><source>Expert Rev Anticancer Ther</source><year>2004</year><volume>4</volume><fpage>151</fpage><lpage>61</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14748665</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="CIT32"><label>32</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nagy</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Schally AV Targeting cytotoxic conjugates of</surname><given-names>somatostatin</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>luteinizing hormone-releasing hormone and bombesin to cancers expressing their receptors: A &#x0201c;smarter&#x0201d; chemotherapy</article-title><source>Curr Pharm Desing</source><year>2005</year><volume>11</volume><fpage>1167</fpage><lpage>80</lpage></element-citation></ref><ref id="CIT33"><label>33</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nagy</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Schally</surname><given-names>AV</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Targeting of citotoxic Luteinizing Hormone Releasing Hormone analogs to breast, ovarian, endometrial and prostate cancers</article-title><source>Biol Reprod</source><year>2005</year><volume>73</volume><fpage>851</fpage><lpage>9</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16033997</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="CIT34"><label>34</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wells</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Souto</surname><given-names>JC</given-names></name><name><surname>Solava</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Kassis</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Bailey</surname><given-names>KJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Turner</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Luteinizing hormone-releasing hormone agonist limits DU-145 prostate cancer growth by attenuating epidermal growth factor receptor signaling</article-title><source>Clin Cancer Res</source><year>2002</year><volume>8</volume><fpage>1251</fpage><lpage>7</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11948140</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list><sec id="sec1-5"><title>AUTHOR'S PROFILE</title><graphic xlink:href="JC-08-51852-g008.jpg" position="float"/><p><bold>Irene Vegh, M.D. Ph.D.</bold></p><p>Born in Debrecen (Hungary). Upon her medical degree in National University of Cordoba in 1971 (Argentina), she join the Medical Faculty at Oncology Institute &#x0201c;AH Roffo&#x0201d; of National University of Buenos Aires, Argentina in 1972. This led to the development of antiestrogen treatment with clomiphene citrate and prolactin regulation and response in breast cancer patient. In 1988 she joined the Research Centre of Hospital Universitario 12 de Octubre, Madrid, Spain, where she upon completion of her doctorate in Internal Medicine &#x02013; Universidad Complutense de Madrid, actually she is working in the same Research Centre as Senior Researcher. She was authored in several publications, and received many awards. Dr. Vegh obtained a personal Spanish Industrial Patent on Tissue Biomarkers Determinations (P -9401877, 1997), and an Intellectual Patent (1994). Concurrently, Dr. Vegh has applied various studies on predictive value of different biomarkers, allelic alterations and its prognostic value in colorectal cancer, non-small cell lung cancer, esophagus cancer. Actually she is working on carcinogenesis based in NMU- induced mammary tumors, their hormonal regulation, and combining basic with translational cancer research.</p></sec></back></article>